<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitution of Cúcuta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1821</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitution of Cúcuta was the constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> Colombia, adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-cucuta">Congress of Cúcuta</a> in 1821. It created a centralised republic joining Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, with a president and vice-president elected indirectly for four years, a two-chamber congress and a high court. Its centralism provoked regional opposition, especially in Venezuela.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-cucuta.jpg" alt="Constitution of Cúcuta" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Congreso de Cúcuta de 1821 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constituci%C3%B3n_pol%C3%ADtica_de_Colombia_de_1821.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
